Q: Reading a WMF header with WINAPI A: <p>You have this structure:</p>
<pre><code>typedef struct _WindowsMetaHeader
{
  uint16_t  FileType;       ///&lt; Type of metafile (0=memory, 1=disk).
  uint16_t  HeaderSize;     ///&lt; Size of header in WORDS (always 9).
  uint16_t  Version;        ///&lt; Version of Microsoft Windows used.
  uint32_t FileSize;       ///&lt; Total size of the metafile in WORDs.
  uint16_t  NumOfObjects;   ///&lt; Number of objects in the file.
  uint32_t MaxRecordSize;  ///&lt; The size of largest record in WORDs.
  uint16_t  NumOfParams;    ///&lt; Not Used (always 0).
} WMFHEAD;
</code></pre>
<p>and you must read item by item and fix endian. You simply cannot read whole structure at once unless you are absolutelly sure that allignment is forced 1byte and structure is packed.</p>
<pre><code>_WindowsMetaHeader HDR;
fread(&amp;HDR.FileType,1,2,FileHandle);
fread(&amp;HDR.HeaderSize,1,2,FileHandle);
fread(&amp;HDR.Version,1,2,FileHandle);
fread(&amp;HDR.FileSize,1,4,FileHandle);
fread(&amp;HDR.NumOfObjects,1,2,FileHandle);
fread(&amp;HDR.MaxRecordSize,1,4,FileHandle);
fread(&amp;HDR.NumOfParams,1,2,FileHandle);
</code></pre>
<p>thats all. Just for WMF detection, follow comments. You need to check items in WMF header. First WORD could be 0 or 1 - nothing else. Second WORD must be 9. NumOfParams must be 0. 6 bytes check gives very high detection reliability. Version is also limited to several numbers and so on.</p>

Q: strspn Strange behavior under c++, why? A: <pre><code>cin &gt;&gt; frase1;
cin &gt;&gt; frase2;
</code></pre>

<p><code>operator&gt;&gt;</code> reads strings word by word, so <code>frase1</code> becomes <code>"veritas"</code> and <code>frase2</code> becomes <code>"omnia"</code>. To read the input line by line use <a href="http://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/io/basic_istream/getline" rel="nofollow"><code>getline</code></a>:</p>

<pre><code>cin.getline(frase1, 100);
cin.getline(frase2, 100);
</code></pre>
